---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site-config';
import { SITES, BRAND } from '../../lib/sites';
import { articleJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.data}/explain/how-to-read-a-spec-sheet/`;
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Explainers', url: '/explain/' },
  { name: 'How to read a spec sheet', url: '/explain/how-to-read-a-spec-sheet/' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  articleJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    headline: 'How to read a spec sheet',
    description:
      'A field-by-field walkthrough of a charcoal specification sheet: what each entry should contain, what a missing field signals, and how to tell a specification from a marketing document.',
    datePublished: '2026-10-07',
    dateModified: '2026-10-07',
  }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.data}/` },
    { name: 'Explainers', url: `${SITES.data}/explain/` },
    { name: 'How to read a spec sheet', url: canonical },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="How to read a spec sheet"
  description="A field-by-field walkthrough of a charcoal specification sheet: what each entry should contain, what a missing field signals, and how to tell a specification from a marketing document."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explainer</p>
      <h1>How to read a spec sheet</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A usable spec sheet names the property, the value, the unit, the method, the basis and
        the batch. When any of those is missing, the value cannot be compared or enforced. This
        page walks through each field.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The anatomy of a usable entry</h2>
      <p>
        Every measured entry should contain six parts. Missing any one of them downgrades the
        entry from a specification to a headline:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Property</strong> — what is being described (ash, moisture, fixed carbon, size…).</li>
        <li><strong>Value</strong> — the number, with a direction (≤, ≥, ±) rather than a bare figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Unit</strong> — %, mm, g, kcal/kg, minutes, as appropriate.</li>
        <li><strong>Method</strong> — the standard or procedure that produced the number.</li>
        <li><strong>Basis</strong> — dry or as-received.</li>
        <li><strong>Batch and laboratory</strong> — which batch, tested by whom, and when.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Field by field</h2>

      <h3>Size and tolerance</h3>
      <p>
        "25 mm" is a nominal target, not a measurement. A usable size entry states the nominal
        dimension <em>and</em> the tolerance (for example ±0.5 mm), plus how conformance is
        checked. Without the tolerance, two suppliers quoting "25 mm" may ship noticeably
        different products — see <a href="/specifications/size-tolerance/">size tolerance</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Moisture</h3>
      <p>
        Moisture should carry a basis (usually as-received) and a drying condition. Because
        charcoal is hygroscopic, a figure measured at origin may not describe the load as
        delivered. Ask when and how the sample was taken — see
        <a href="/specifications/moisture/">moisture</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Ash</h3>
      <p>
        Ash should carry the method and basis. Different methods ash at different temperatures,
        so ash figures are only comparable within a method family — see
        <a href="/specifications/ash-content/">ash content</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Fixed carbon</h3>
      <p>
        Fixed carbon is calculated by difference, so check that it reconciles with the reported
        moisture, volatile matter and ash on the same basis. If it does not, the sheet is
        internally inconsistent — see <a href="/specifications/fixed-carbon/">fixed carbon</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Performance claims (burn time, ignition time, odour)</h3>
      <p>
        These have no universal standard. A burn-time figure is only meaningful with its test
        conditions attached — fuel mass, apparatus, ignition method, airflow and endpoint. Treat
        a bare "60 minutes" as a scenario, not a property — see
        <a href="/specifications/burning-time/">burning time</a> and
        <a href="/specifications/ignition-time/">ignition time</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What a missing field signals</h2>
      <p>
        A missing method or basis is the most common red flag. It is usually not malice — it is
        often a marketing datasheet that was never written to be enforced. The remedy is the
        same in every case: ask for the raw report, and confirm the laboratory is independent of
        the supplier.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-specs-are-measured/">How specifications are measured</a></li>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-to-compare/">How to compare two suppliers</a></li>
        <li><a href="/explain/supplier-reported-vs-verified/">Supplier-reported vs verified</a></li>
        <li><a href="/specifications/">All specification methodology pages</a></li>
        <li><a href="/products/">Product records</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
